    "memo": " BILL NUMBER:  S5779A\n\n TITLE OF BILL :  An act to amend the insurance law, in relation to\nrequiring health insurers to provide, when liability for a claim is\nnot reasonably clear, the coverage the policyholder or covered person\nis enrolled in\n\n PURPOSE :\n\nThe require plans to clearly identify to a provider the product an\nindividual is enrolled in for health coverage purposes.\n\n SUMMARY OF PROVISIONS :\n\nSection 1 amends subsection (b) of paragraph (2) of section 3224-a of\nthe Insurance Law to add a new requirement that an insurer identify\nthe specific type of plan or product the policy-holder or covered\nperson is enrolled in.\n\nSection 2 amends subsection (d) of section 3224-a of the Insurance law\nto add a new paragraph 3 to define the plans or products that must be\nidentified by the insurer.\n\nSection 3 provides that this act shall take effect July 1, 2018.\n\n JUSTIFICATION :\n\nHospitals and health care providers are currently challenged with\nidentifying which specific health plan a patient is enrolled in.\nHealth plans today offer a wide variety of products, which can make it\nvery difficult to identify what particular health coverage a patient\nhas. There is no requirement for plans to consistently and uniformly\nidentify what particular product an individual is enrolled in.\n\nThe current inability to identify these products or the regulatory\nrules that apply causes providers, regulators, and plans to waste\nvaluable resources appealing, communicating, and replacing claims\nincorrectly or when it is not necessary. Under the current system,\nidentification of the type of coverage is ultimately made after an\nappeal is submitted by a patient or a provider and after these\nresources have already been wasted.\n\nThis bill would ensure plans provide the necessary information at the\ntime the plan makes a payment determination. This will avoid wasting\ntime and energy on filing unnecessary appeals and identify protections\navailable for patients based on their particular coverage by ensuring\nclear and consistent information about the particular product a\npatient is enrolled in.\n\n LEGISLATIVE HISTORY :\n\n2016: S.7370\n\n FISCAL IMPLICATIONS :\n\nNone\n\n EFFECTIVE DATE :\nJuly 1, 2018\n",
